{"note":"V9's East Turkestan rows, province-history units and country note as of Version 218 (kept for tools/v9_et_back220.py)","row":{"id":"156_X","n":"China: East Turkestan as its government in exile defines it (Xinjiang without Aksai Chin; Subei, Aksay, Dunhuang, Guazhou in Gansu; Lenghu and Mangnai in Qinghai)","km2":1784726,"pct":19,"core":false,"hs":{"dynasty":80,"islamic":19}},"units":[{"a3":"XJ","country":"China (Xinjiang)","label":"The Tarim basin (Altishahr: Kashgar, Yarkand, Khotan, Aksu, Kucha)","cat":"dynasty","conf":"medium","who":"The Karakhanids, who recognised the Abbasid caliph (Kashgar from the 10th century, Khotan from about 1006); then Yettishar under Yaqub Beg, who gave his allegiance to the Ottoman sultan-caliph in 1873 and struck coins in his name","dates":"about 1006 to the 1130s in the west (Karakhanid); 1873–1877 (Yettishar under the Ottoman caliph)","nominal":true,"states":[{"n":"Karakhanids","d":"10th century–1130s (then under Qara Khitai overlordship)","r":"yes"},{"n":"Chagatai khans and Moghulistan","d":"14th–15th centuries","r":"no"},{"n":"Yarkand Khanate","d":"1514–1705","r":"no"},{"n":"Yettishar (Yaqub Beg)","d":"1864/65–1877; under the Ottoman caliph from 1873","r":"yes"}],"notes":"Kashgar was a Karakhanid capital and Khotan was taken from its Buddhist kings about 1006; the eastern oases (Kucha, Korla) came under Muslim rule later. Yaqub Beg made Kashgar his capital in 1865; in 1873 he offered his allegiance to the Ottoman sultan ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz as caliph, received the title of Emir, and struck coins in his name. He died at Korla in 1877, and the Qing retook the region by 1878. The tie to a caliph was nominal both times.","uncertain":"From general knowledge, not checked against the sources: the dates of the Chagatai, Moghulistan and Yarkand khanates, and the Qing conquest of 1759. The search found no source for the Karakhanids’ recognition of the Abbasids beyond the one used for southern Kazakhstan in this table.","sources":[{"t":"Kara-Khanid Khanate (Wikipedia)","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kara-Khanid_Khanate","k":"modern","s":"the first Turkic Muslim dynasty; Kashgar one of its capitals"},{"t":"The Qarakhanid campaign against Khotan (Study Buddhism)","u":"https://studybuddhism.com/en/advanced-studies/history-culture/buddhism-islam-advanced/buddhist-muslim-interaction-later-abbasid-period/qarakhanid-campaign-against-khotan","k":"modern","s":"Khotan taken about 1006 and absorbed into the Qarakhanid realm"},{"t":"Yettishar (Wikipedia)","u":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yettishar","k":"modern","s":"Yaqub Beg’s state in the Tarim basin, 1864/65–1877; Kashgar its capital"},{"t":"Coin of Yaqub Beg naming the Ottoman sultan (NumisBids lot note)","u":"https://www.numisbids.com/sale/8237/lot/1601","k":"modern","s":"allegiance to the Ottoman sultan-caliph ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz in 1873, the title of Emir in return; ruled Yangi Hisar, Yarkand, Khotan, Aqsu, Kucha, Korla, Turfan, Urumqi and Kara Sheher; died at Korla in 1877"},{"t":"Yaqub Beg coins dated from 1873 in the name of ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz (Spink)","u":"https://www.spink.com/lot/CSS36001508","k":"modern","s":"named on a series of coins from 1873"}],"km2":547264,"c":[-100.171,-38.601],"polys":[[[[73.602,39.236],[73.605,39.22],[73.65,39.175],[73.675,39.15],[73.7,39.125],[73.7,39.077],[73.709,39.066],[73.769,39.03],[73.801,39.001],[73.803,39],[73.825,39],[73.85,38.975],[73.826,38.951],[73.824,38.945],[73.809,38.932],[73.788,38.927],[73.752,38.927],[73.75,38.925],[73.725,38.9],[73.7,38.875],[73.7,38.837],[73.725,38.815],[73.736,38.8],[73.738,38.787],[73.75,38.775],[73.75,38.703],[73.772,38.674],[73.779,38.66],[73.783,38.642],[73.8,38.625],[73.825,38.6],[73.85,38.575],[73.853,38.572],[73.939,38.572],[73.96,38.55],[74.025,38.55],[74.045,38.57],[74.037,38.579],[74.037,38.598],[74.045,38.603],[74.084,38.614],[74.091,38.62],[74.094,38.629],[74.092,38.637],[74.095,38.646],[74.11,38.662],[74.123,38.67],[74.14,38.67],[74.144,38.669],[74.202,38.653],[74.222,38.65],[